    {
      "path": "work/sources/openai-math/preprints/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026/build/main.tex",
      "sha256": "5671be265cb2b1b1acbd0e36c10fde90660a812a15efc3d00fa5bd743927027b",
      "bytes": 11019,
      "line_count": 118,
      "anchor_lines": [
        {
          "line": 85,
          "text": "Fix $n\\geq3$. We begin with a freely generating Haar tuple $(A_1,\\ldots,A_n,C)$ in $L(\\F_{n+1})$ and set $S=\\arg(C)$ using bounded Borel functional calculus. For $g\\in\\F_n$, let $A_g$ denote the corresponding word in $A_1,\\ldots,A_n$. Conjugates $A_gSA_g^*$ drive a polynomial differential equation in $C^*(A_1,\\ldots,A_n,S)$. A formal trace identity and analyticity of the actual solutions' moments prove moment preservation. The resulting maps descend through the relations among the generators, preserve the operator norm, and extend to normal trace-preserving automorphisms."
        },
        {
          "line": 101,
          "text": "A free-group basis change now yields an automorphism moving each $A_j$ arbitrarily little in operator norm while making a word in the moved $A_j$'s approximate the \\emph{old} variable $C$; this is Proposition~\\ref{prop:perturbation}. We iterate this step, choosing each future error budget only after the current approximating word has been fixed. The first $n$ coordinates converge in norm, so their joint Haar distribution persists. The complementary coordinate may vary at every stage. Generation is proved separately: the word algebra of the limiting tuple is dense in the ambient tracial $L^2$ space, and the trace-preserving conditional expectation then identifies its von Neumann closure with the entire factor."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "path": "work/sources/openai-math/preprints/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex",
      "sha256": "045afbe09efe71dbeff815a207dcfb6bb94ba8c68f99532c7b96449323a70dcb",
      "bytes": 10142,
      "line_count": 115,
      "anchor_lines": [
        {
          "line": 44,
          "text": "S=\\arg(C),\\qquad S_g=A_gSA_g^*\\quad(g\\in\\Gamma),"
        },
        {
          "line": 48,
          "text": "S=S^*,\\quad \\norm{S}\\leq\\pi,\\quad \\tau(S)=0,\\quad e^{\\ii S}=C,"
        },
        {
          "line": 71,
          "text": "For the second assertion, successively use $A_g S=S_g A_g$ to move every group factor to the right. The final group factor is exactly the label, and is therefore $1$."
        }
      ]
    },
